WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.130
Aaron Swartz was nothing short of a child
prodigy.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.889
At the age of 14, he helped create RSS, a
popular tool for keeping up with the latest

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:12.710
news and blog posts before the advent of social
media.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:16.640
He formed a company that merged with Reddit
and is credited with making Reddit one of

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.109
the most popular sites on the internet.

00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:21.369
Yet that’s not what he was known for.

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:26.130
He was known for his internet activism, which
led to the ultimate tragedy.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.940
I couldn’t wait until the whole world was
on the internet and I never had to call anybody

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:31.940
again.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.930
The computer was his favorite toy growing
up in a wealthy suburb of Chicago.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.390
His father worked in the tech industry, so
computers were always a part of his life.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.610
They were also a distraction from the serious
bowel condition that often crippled him with

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.110
stomach pain.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:50.000
As a result, he only ate bland food like rice
and pasta.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.350
He hated fruits and veggies.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.270
Aaron thought the internet was magical.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.210
One of the exciting things about Wikipedia
is that it doesn’t just have an article

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.469
on the 100 most popular things, or the thousand
most popular things.

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:04.910
You can pick the most obscure subject on the
world and there’s an article about it.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.770
Because for everything there’s someone who
cares a great deal about it.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.580
And that’s what television, that’s what
radio doesn’t provide, but the internet

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.580
does.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.760
Yet he also felt there were barriers that
limited the ability of people to share and

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.430
distribute knowledge freely, like strict copyright
laws.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.970
We have the right as American citizens to
have this sort of free and open communication

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.729
to share what we think, what we feel, what
we’ve created.

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:30.700
I think that’s a huge part of our culture.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.790
This notion that we’ve become a permission-asking
society that every time you do something you

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:36.689
have to ask permission.

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:40.180
That’s basically against the freedom culture
we have here.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.540
That’s the opposite of a free country.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.240
Aaron believed no one should be deprived of
information and sought to break down these

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.240
barriers.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.600
He dropped out of Stanford after a year to
pursue his interest in internet freedom.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.009
In 2008, he wrote this manifesto:

00:01:55.009 --> 00:01:56.909
“Information is power.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:01.039
But like all power, there are those who want
to keep it for themselves.

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:05.680
The world’s entire scientific and cultural
heritage, published over centuries in books

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.989
and journals, is increasingly being digitized
and locked up by a handful of private corporations.

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:16.980
We need to take information, wherever it is
stored, make our copies and share them with

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:17.980
the world.”

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.930
That year, he took on the U.S. government’s
online court records system, PACER, which

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:27.480
stores information about cases, court filings,
and docket details.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:32.910
He was frustrated that PACER charged for documents
-- 8 cents per page at the time – 10 cents

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:33.910
today.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.700
Aaron believed the documents should be free
because they were produced at the taxpayer’s

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.700
expense.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.970
So he set out to republish these documents
on a public website for anyone to access.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:49.170
He visited one of the few public libraries
that did allow free downloads of PACER documents

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.830
and installed code he'd written to request
a new document every three seconds.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:59.599
This way, he got his hands on nearly 20 million
pages of documents - or around 20% of the

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:03.799
database, and uploaded the files to Amazon’s
cloud computing service.

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:10.060
When the court system’s IT department realized
something was up, the FBI got involved, and

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.840
contacted Amazon, which provided his name,
phone number, and address as their user agreement

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.020
allows them to do so at the government’s
request.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:22.560
Aaron recalled in an interview, “I had this
vision of the feds crashing down the door,

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.470
taking everything away.”

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.280
The feds investigated but didn’t prosecute
him.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.780
Two years later, it would be a different story…when
authorities say he hacked the servers at MIT

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:36.980
to steal millions of files from a digital
library.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:42.209
In September 2010, federal authorities say
Aaron broke into MIT’s computer network

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:48.319
by accessing a restricted utility closet on
campus in the basement of Building 16, which

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:50.700
housed network equipment and cabling.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.129
The room was reportedly left unlocked.

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:58.099
He hardwired his laptop into the network and
hid it under a cardboard box.

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:04.340
The laptop ran a script called “keepgrabbing.py”
which set it to automatically download academic

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.410
journals from the digital library JSTOR.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.150
Aaron used a guest account to access JSTOR.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.099
At the time, any computer connected to MIT’s
open wireless network could access the digital

00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:16.430
library.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:20.829
Many institutions pay a lot of money so their
students can subscribe for free.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:27.460
Otherwise, plans for individuals cost $19.50
a month for just 10 PDF downloads.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.840
He created the fake guest identity “Gary
Host” which prompted the computer network

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.570
to identify his computer as “ghost” when
shortened - an apparent reference to its ability

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.420
to disappear.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:43.330
Although Aaron used a legitimate guest account,
he violated JSTOR’s terms of use by using

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.840
an automated program to access articles.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.509
At one point, he downloaded about 700 articles
a minute!

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:57.360
Between September 2010 to January 2011, he
downloaded nearly 5 million articles or 80%

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.110
of JSTOR’s database, valued at hundreds
of thousands of dollars.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:06.919
A federal indictment claims he intended to
upload all of the documents to a peer-to-peer

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.440
file-sharing site where anyone could access
them for free.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.380
But he never got the chance.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:17.780
JSTOR noticed the huge spike in downloads
and alerted MIT, which launched an investigation

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:22.470
alongside the Cambridge Police Department
and the U.S. Secret Service.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:27.350
Blocking the IP address from which the requests
originated didn’t work; the person responsible

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.860
simply switched IPs to continue the mass downloading.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.270
A second computer then connected to MIT’s
network.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:39.190
Both laptops downloaded articles at such a
staggering rate that several of JSTOR’s

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.539
servers crashed.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:45.300
JSTOR then blocked access to all MIT users
for several days.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:51.240
On January 4, 2011, authorities discovered
a laptop hidden in the utility closet in Building

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.240
16.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.110
They decided to leave the laptop in place
to see if the person who put it there would

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.110
return.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:01.539
They also installed a hidden camera in the
closet to identify the intruder.

00:06:01.539 --> 00:06:06.950
30 minutes later, Aaron is captured on video
entering the closet to purportedly replace

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:08.470
the hard drive.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:12.960
The video you’re watching was originally
published by Wired.com, which obtained it

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.660
through a Freedom of Information Act lawsuit
against the U.S. Secret Service.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:23.380
Two days later, on January 6, 2011, someone
entered the closet again, covering their face

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:24.520
with a bike helmet.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.190
They removed the laptop and hard drive and
left.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.530
The MIT police weren’t able to reach the
closet in time to apprehend the person.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:35.130
But the suspect didn’t leave the campus
immediately.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:38.520
The same laptop then connected to MIT’s
Student Center.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.199
Perhaps to access the network from a different
location to avoid detection.

00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:47.810
Later that afternoon, an MIT police officer
who saw the surveillance footage recognized

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:50.860
the same guy riding his bike on campus.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.050
When the officer approached Aaron, Aaron leaped
off his bike and ran away.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:07:00.509
But MIT police and a secret service agent
caught up with him and arrested him for breaking

00:07:00.509 --> 00:07:01.750
and entering.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:07.580
At the time of his arrest, Aaron was found
to possess a USB drive containing the program

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:12.040
“keepgrabbing.py” - the same software
program found on the laptop in the closet.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.330
There was no doubt they had the right person.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:21.460
The U.S. Attorney’s Office in Boston charged
Aaron with numerous counts of wire fraud and

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.539
violating the Computer Fraud and Abuse act.

00:07:24.539 --> 00:07:31.020
The charges carried a maximum sentence of
35 years in prison and a fine of up to $1

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.020
million.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:36.479
His supporters called it ludicrous; they argued
he simply wanted to make academic research

00:07:36.479 --> 00:07:38.080
more widely available.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:41.250
They believed the punishment didn’t fit
the crime.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:46.690
However, Carmen Ortiz, the then U.S. Attorney
for the District of Massachusetts who oversaw

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:48.590
Aaron’s prosecution, argued:

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:53.629
“Stealing is stealing, whether you use a
computer command or a crowbar, and whether

00:07:53.629 --> 00:07:56.870
you take documents, data or dollars.”

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:01.530
JSTOR chose NOT to press charges after Aaron
turned over his hard drives.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.220
The prosecution offered Aaron a plea deal.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:08.120
Had he accepted it, he would have gone to
prison for only six months.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:13.050
His girlfriend Taren reflected on the difficult
decision he had to make.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:16.759
The whole thing was so hard and so stressful.

00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:21.629
And he felt - he carried so much of the weight
of it on his own.

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:24.409
He didn’t want to involve his friends.

00:08:24.409 --> 00:08:28.570
He wanted to protect people but he wasn’t
very good at protecting himself.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:33.159
Aaron refused the plea bargain because he
didn’t want to be labeled a felon.

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:37.610
A close friend and former girlfriend recalled
to Rolling Stone how they were standing outside

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:42.089
the White House one day when Aaron turned
to her and said, “‘You know, they don’t

00:08:42.089 --> 00:08:44.850
allow felons to work there.'”

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:48.430
At the time he was charged, he had been a
research fellow at Harvard.

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:52.459
Harvard then suspended his fellowship and
banned him from campus.

00:08:52.459 --> 00:08:56.370
Aaron settled into a studio apartment in Brooklyn,
awaiting trial.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:59.089
He grew increasingly isolated and depressed.

00:08:59.089 --> 00:09:04.800
He had struggled for years with depression,
often triggered by his stomach issues.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.320
Long before the JSTOR incident, he wrote a
blog on his personal website that described

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:10.700
his state of mind:

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:15.260
“Go outside and get some fresh air or cuddle
with a loved one and you don’t feel any

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:21.380
better, only more upset at being unable to
feel the joy that everyone else seems to feel.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.230
Everything gets colored by the sadness.”

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:28.800
“You feel as if streaks of pain are running
through your head, you thrash your body, you

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.680
search for some escape but find none.”

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:39.070
On January 11, 2013, Aaron Swartz was found
dead in his apartment in New York City.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:44.519
He was 26 years old.

00:09:44.519 --> 00:09:49.900
I think he woke up two years after this ordeal
started, and I think he couldn’t face another

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:55.470
day of the stress, the uncertainty, the lack
of control over his own destiny.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:10:00.670
Aaron’s partner and family blamed his death
on prosecutors, declaring in a statement:

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:03.930
“Aaron’s death is not simply a personal
tragedy.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:11.450
It is the product of a criminal justice system
rife with intimidation and prosecutorial overreach.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:16.820
Decisions made by officials in the Massachusetts
U.S. Attorney’s Office and at MIT contributed

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.709
to his death.”

00:10:18.709 --> 00:10:22.420
There were calls for U.S. Attorney Carmen
Ortiz to be removed.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:25.140
She defended her handling of the case this
way:

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:30.280
“...this office sought an appropriate sentence
that matched the alleged conduct – a sentence

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:34.110
that we would recommend to the judge of six
months in a low security setting.

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:37.899
While at the same time, his defense counsel
would have been free to recommend a sentence

00:10:37.899 --> 00:10:38.899
of probation.

00:10:38.899 --> 00:10:39.899
Ultimately, any sentence imposed would have
been up to the judge.

00:10:39.899 --> 00:10:44.070
At no time did this office ever seek – or
ever tell Mr. Swartz’s attorneys that it

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:49.120
intended to seek – maximum penalties under
the law.”

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.660
Aaron leaves a lasting legacy.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:56.399
He was an early contributor to the development
of the Creative Commons license, which provides

00:10:56.399 --> 00:11:00.700
a legal framework for creators to share their
work with others under certain conditions.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:05.779
It has since become an important tool for
promoting open access to information – his

00:11:05.779 --> 00:11:07.580
goal all along.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:10.329
I have personally benefited from the use of
creative commons.

00:11:10.329 --> 00:11:15.110
If I couldn’t access this vast library of
free and open content, I would have had to

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:20.840
spend much more time sourcing material, making
videos like this one much more difficult to

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:21.840
produce.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.560
Aaron’s belief in limiting the power of
institutions has influenced debates on free

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.160
speech and whether social media companies
have gone too far in censoring information.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.730
He had co-founded an advocacy group dedicated
to protecting civil liberties and fighting

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.139
censorship.

00:11:38.139 --> 00:11:41.180
People often ask me, what’s going to happen?

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:42.281
And the answer is: it’s up to you.

00:11:42.281 --> 00:11:44.630
You get to decide what will happen.

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:48.310
This isn’t something playing out on a stage
somewhere where big giants fight each other,

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:50.149
and you get to sit and munch popcorn.

00:11:50.149 --> 00:11:51.840
This is a fight you can join in.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:55.570
It’s up to you.

00:11:55.570 --> 00:12:00.760
The case against Aaron wasn’t the first
of its kind to challenge online civil disobedience.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.400
The same year of Aaron’s death, Ross Ulbricht
was arrested and charged with running the

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:08.630
dark web online marketplace Silk Road.

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:13.250
The harsh sentence Ross received has been
the topic of widespread discussion.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:17.950
You can check out my video on that, which
I’ve linked in my description.

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:22.490
The computer science skills of Aaron Swartz
and Ross Ulbricht played a huge role in their

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.520
attempt to change the world.

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:29.620
Although their actions led to legal consequences,
their stories demonstrate the power that STEM

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:31.930
can have to make an impact.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:36.850
Well, Brilliant.org is a website and app where
you can learn science, computer science, and

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:38.589
math interactively.

00:12:38.589 --> 00:12:41.530
My viewers especially love their computer
science courses.

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:45.680
They’re great whether you’re starting
out or if you want to brush up on your skills.

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:49.900
You can dive deeper into more advanced topics
as you progress through their lessons.

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:52.420
Brilliant supports you every step of the way.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:56.070
If you’re stuck, you can view the explanation
to see where you went wrong.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:13:00.290
There are thousands of lessons to choose from,
everything from foundational and advanced

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.110
math to data science.

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:07.889
I personally use Brilliant to go through their
logic puzzles to improve my analytical thinking.

00:13:07.889 --> 00:13:14.720
Brilliant is FREE for you to try out for 30
